  {
    "episode_id": 85713,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/5/85713.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "I think there is a consensus that there has been too much regulation of small and medium-sized banks, which really were not the cause of the financial crisis that are unlikely to be systematically important and cause failure around. And so we've had very, very few new small banks start and a lot of them would tell you that they're overwhelmed by regulations on money laundering and just all sorts of detail stuff that really causes great compliance costs. So I think that will change. The one thing that Frank didn't do is they did not address Fannie and Freddie. These are the two previously shareholder-owned institutions, and they continue to buy mortgages, but they're owned by the federal government now effectively because they're in conservatorship. Everybody would like to change them, but the difference of opinion is used. Some people would like to get rid of them totally and let the mortgages go back to the normal market others will say well what we need is to turn them into government agencies and stop making believe that they're owned by shareholders so there still a tremendous disagreement on that point and i'm not sure that that's going to be resolved sooner than later",
