  {
    "episode_id": 85713,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/5/85713.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. Well, and you're right. I agree with you completely. I just want to say that the counter argument to that is that, you know, that'll reduce competition in the marketplace. It'll increase cost for consumers, et cetera. I mean, you know what I'm going to say there, right? So it's a tough line to walk, isn't it?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "I agree with you completely.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Reducing competition in the marketplace leads to increased costs for consumers.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "The counterargument to your position is that reducing competition will increase consumer costs.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a difficult balance to maintain between competing interests.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that maintaining market competition is valuable.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views policy decisions as requiring trade-offs between competing outcomes.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker assumes the listener understands the economic implications of reduced competition.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener shares similar values regarding market efficiency and consumer welfare.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker trusts their own judgment on economic consequences.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 85713,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/5/85713.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, no, I think there's all a question of what's a reasonable balance. And the answer is that we have lots of banks that originate mortgages that have capital. And I'm perfectly okay with lots of other types of institutions that originate mortgages. But if a mortgage broker originates a lot of mortgages and sells 100% of them, to the federal government or to for any may, then they no longer have a stake in that mortgage really being done properly. So I think it's, if you don't want to have capital, then you can have some risk or retention or some representations or something. But you've got to, you've got, it is a moral hazard to tell somebody, you can make money by issuing a mortgage, but then You can sell the mortgage the day after 100% of it and you no longer care enough about what happens to the mortgage.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Telling someone they can profit from issuing a mortgage while selling it entirely immediately creates a moral hazard.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "There is a question of what constitutes a reasonable balance regarding mortgage origination.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "A mortgage broker who originates many mortgages and sells 100% of them does not retain a meaningful stake in their proper execution.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Many banks that originate mortgages possess capital.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "If an institution chooses not to hold capital, it should still bear some form of risk through retention, representation, or similar mechanisms.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "It is acceptable for various types of institutions to originate mortgages.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Moral hazard arises when incentives misalign between creating a product and bearing its consequences.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Mortgage originators should have a financial stake in the quality of the loans they issue.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The absence of a personal financial stake leads to reduced diligence in loan underwriting.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Institutions that do not hold capital cannot be trusted to act responsibly without external constraints.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Financial systems require accountability mechanisms beyond mere transactional activity.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 85713,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/5/85713.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely, no question. The whole system was incentivized improperly. I remember reading an article in one of the major magazines about that in circa 2002 or 2003. I've owned a couple of mortgage companies over the years. It's just unbelievable. I mean, these mortgage brokers, so many of them, I would just say it. I mean, they're such crooks. They'll just",
