    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/5/85713.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 67,
    "turn_text": "Well, there are rules now that constrain that. They don't prevent it, but they constrain it in the sense that depending on what level you are, if you leave the SEC, you're not allowed to work for a year or two on any matter that you had participated in when you're at the SEC. And that goes for a lot of other agencies. And then there are certain bans on other people about lobbying an agency. where you were very senior official. But having said all that, those are short-term constraints. And after a year or two, you can do what you want. But you know that's a double-edged sword because if we were a country like say France, there are all the bureaucrats stay in the government for years. They're all go to a certain number of universities and get trained. And there's very little intersection here. I think it's actually a good thing. that we have people in the government who have private experience and then people who come from the government who then go in the private sector. So to some degree, that's a positive. But I agree with you. We have to be worried about conflicts of",
