    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/5/85713.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 70,
    "turn_text": "Well, the biggest things are, first of all, that we should increase the capital requirements of financial institutions, which we pretty much done. And then probably the biggest thing that we didn't think about was liquidity. Equity turns out to be much more important than people. previously thought, you may have an asset that's good, but the question is, if you get a financial crisis, can you really sell it? So now we have more rules that require financial institutions to hold the liquid assets. And I think that's a second thing that's very important. And the third thing is we have a very broad range of institutions that are under the radar and a good one now is online lenders. So a lot of these online lenders and they become very big and they're lending and the question is, you know, is there a regulator for them? Who are they lending to? What are the rates? What are the You know, so it's, again, a two-edged sword. We want to encourage lending. We want to encourage innovation. But sometimes I wonder whether these people who are giving their money to various portals. for new businesses and online lending. Sometimes I wonder whether these people really know what they're getting into.",
