    "turn_idx": 77,
    "turn_text": "in the crowdfunding area, the question is, do people, you know, it's okay with me if people want to take a of flying and trying a new business. I don't think a lot of these people realize that most of these new businesses go belly up and the question is, are people really knowledgeable about the risk that they're taking? So that's the issue with crowdfunding. Most of these crowdfunding companies don't have detailed business plans. They don't have any financials and so it's perfectly okay with me. If a sophisticated investors put their money in, then they either lose it or not. But I hate to see people who are in a position where they really can't afford to lose the money, lose it when they really don't know what risk they're getting in. The peer-to-peer lending is a separate question. They're lending now to a lot of small businesses, medium-sized businesses, probably partly because of the thing we just talked about where small, medium-sized banks aren't willing to serve these people. So they're getting their loans from peer-to-peer lending. They're playing very high rates. Again, we have to question, you know, are these things being done in a proper manner? They're pretty much unregulated at the moment, but there are talk about having some oversight.",
